[242/1873] Denominator_Calibrated_Living_NMA
TITLE: Denominator-Calibrated Living NMA: Registry-First Network Meta-Analysis with Sample Size Adjustment
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: Denominator-calibrated pooled treatment ranking
DATA: ClinicalTrials.gov registry data with enrollment denominators for NMA networks
PATH: C:\Models\Denominator_Calibrated_Living_NMA

CURRENT BODY (154 words):
Can network meta-analysis rankings be calibrated against registry enrollment denominators to detect when pooled comparisons rely on disproportionately small evidence fractions? We developed a denominator-calibrated NMA framework augmenting contrast-based network meta-analysis with registry-derived ClinicalTrials.gov enrollment to weight comparisons by evidence mass. The calibration applies an enrollment-adjusted information fraction to each network edge, penalising comparisons where the pooled sample is under 20 percent of registered enrollment for that pair. Treatment rankings shifted for 3 of 12 comparisons in the test network when denominator calibration was applied, with the largest shift occurring where the published evidence covered only 8 percent of registered enrollment. Living updates monitor new registry entries and flag when published evidence fractions change by more than 5 percentage points. Denominator calibration could alert users when NMA rankings are driven by selectively published subsets of registered evidence. The framework depends on accurate ClinicalTrials.gov enrollment reporting and cannot detect unpublished trials that were never registered.

YOUR REWRITE (at most 156 words, 7 sentences):



Can network meta-analysis rankings be calibrated against registry-reported enrollment denominators to detect when comparisons rely on disproportionately small evidence fractions? We developed a denominator-calibrated NMA framework augmenting standard contrast-based network meta-analysis with registry-derived enrollment data from ClinicalTrials.gov to weight comparisons by evidence mass. The calibration applies an enrollment-adjusted information fraction to each network edge, penalising comparisons where the pooled sample represents less than 20 percent of registered enrollment. Treatment rankings shifted for 3 of 12 comparisons in the test network when calibration was applied, with the largest shift occurring where published evidence covered only 8 percent of registered enrollment. Living update functionality monitors new registry entries and flags when published evidence fractions change by more than 5 percentage points. Denominator calibration could alert users when NMA rankings are driven by selectively published subsets of registered evidence. The framework depends on accurate ClinicalTrials.gov enrollment reporting and cannot detect unregistered trials.

