[246/1873] EvidenceOracle
TITLE: EvidenceOracle: Machine Learning Prediction of Meta-Analytic Result Instability
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: AUC for predicting meta-analysis instability
DATA: Features from 6,229 Cochrane meta-analyses with temporal instability labels
PATH: C:\Models\EvidenceOracle

CURRENT BODY (153 words):
Can machine-learning models predict which meta-analytic conclusions are likely to change when new studies are added, enabling proactive surveillance? We trained gradient-boosted classifiers on 42 features from 6,229 Cochrane meta-analyses, labelling each stable or unstable by whether the pooled estimate direction or significance changed in the next Cochrane update. EvidenceOracle extracts features from heterogeneity metrics, fragility indices, publication bias indicators, study count, and cumulative Z-statistic trajectories to generate instability scores. The model achieved AUC 0.925 (95% CI 0.91 to 0.94) on held-out data, the top five features being fragility index, cumulative Z-slope, study count, I-squared trend, and small-study effect magnitude. Calibration confirmed predicted probabilities aligned with observed instability rates across deciles within 3 percentage points. Proactive instability prediction could focus living evidence surveillance resources on meta-analyses most likely to change with new evidence. The model was trained on Cochrane reviews and prediction accuracy may differ for non-Cochrane systematic reviews with different methodological standards.

YOUR REWRITE (at most 156 words, 7 sentences):



Can machine learning predict which meta-analytic conclusions are likely to change when new studies are added? We trained gradient-boosted classifiers on 42 features from 6,229 Cochrane meta-analyses, labelling each as stable or unstable based on whether the pooled estimate changed in the subsequent Cochrane update. EvidenceOracle extracts features from heterogeneity metrics, fragility indices, publication bias indicators, study count, and cumulative Z-statistic trajectories to generate instability risk scores. The model achieved an AUC of 0.925 (95% CI 0.91 to 0.94) on held-out test data, with top predictive features being fragility index, cumulative Z-slope, number of studies, I-squared trend, and small-study effect magnitude. Calibration analysis confirmed that predicted probabilities aligned with observed instability rates across deciles within 3 percentage points. Proactive instability prediction could focus living evidence surveillance on meta-analyses most likely to change with new evidence. The model was trained on Cochrane reviews and accuracy may differ for non-Cochrane reviews with different methodological standards.

