[247/1873] experimental-meta-analysis
TITLE: Experimental Meta-Analysis Framework: 300+ Methods with Simulation Infrastructure
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: Method performance metrics (coverage, MSE, bias)
DATA: Simulated meta-analysis datasets across 300+ method configurations
PATH: C:\Projects\experimental-meta-analysis

CURRENT BODY (155 words):
Can a unified simulation framework compare over 300 meta-analysis methods to identify which perform best under specific data-generating conditions? We built a Python framework implementing heterogeneity estimators, confidence interval methods, prediction intervals, bias correction, robust variance estimation, and model selection with automated simulation infrastructure. The framework generates datasets varying true effect size, heterogeneity, study number, sample size distribution, and publication bias, then scores each method on coverage, mean squared error, bias, and computational cost. Across 18 simulation scenarios the Hartung-Knapp interval with REML achieved the best coverage, 94.8 percent (95% CI 94.5 to 95.1), while DerSimonian-Laird dropped to 88.2 percent under high heterogeneity. Method rankings were sensitive to the number of studies, with several modern approaches outperforming classical methods only when k exceeded 20. Systematic simulation comparison could guide methodologists toward optimal choices for specific synthesis contexts. The framework tests frequentist properties under normal-normal assumptions and does not yet cover Bayesian or non-parametric meta-analysis approaches.

YOUR REWRITE (at most 156 words, 7 sentences):


Can a unified simulation framework systematically compare over 300 meta-analysis methods to identify which approaches perform best under specific conditions? We built a Python framework implementing heterogeneity estimators, confidence interval methods, prediction intervals, bias correction, robust variance estimation, and model selection with automated simulation infrastructure. The framework generates datasets under controlled conditions varying effect size, heterogeneity, number of studies, sample size distribution, and publication bias, then evaluates each method on coverage, mean squared error, and bias. Across 18 primary scenarios the Hartung-Knapp interval with REML estimation achieved best overall coverage of 94.8 percent (95% CI 94.5 to 95.1) while DerSimonian-Laird dropped to 88.2 percent under high heterogeneity. Method rankings were sensitive to the number of studies, with several modern approaches outperforming classical methods only when k exceeded 20. Systematic simulation comparison could guide methodologists toward optimal method choices for specific evidence synthesis contexts. The framework tests frequentist properties under normal-normal assumptions and does not yet cover Bayesian approaches.

