[248/1873] Fatiha-Course
TITLE: Advanced Meta-Analysis Course: A 25-Module Interactive Educational Platform
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: Module completion and quiz performance metrics
DATA: 25 teaching modules with interactive exercises and assessment quizzes
PATH: C:\Projects\Fatiha-Course

CURRENT BODY (154 words):
Can an interactive browser course teach advanced meta-analysis from fixed-effects pooling through network meta-analysis with enough depth for independent research? We developed a 3,276-line educational platform comprising 25 modules covering effect sizes, random-effects models, heterogeneity assessment, publication bias, meta-regression, diagnostic test accuracy meta-analysis, network meta-analysis, and individual participant data methods. Each module provides worked numerical examples with step-by-step derivations, interactive parameter exploration, quiz assessment with immediate feedback, and links to the corresponding browser-based computational tools in the portfolio. The course was validated by checking all numerical examples against R metafor output with agreement within rounding tolerance across all 25 modules. Module quizzes included distractor options based on common misconceptions identified from published meta-analysis methodology surveys and teaching evaluations. A comprehensive interactive course could make advanced meta-analysis accessible to researchers without programming skills or statistical software. The course covers methods conceptually and computationally but cannot replace supervised application with real datasets and experienced methodological mentorship.

YOUR REWRITE (at most 156 words, 7 sentences):


Can an interactive browser-based course teach advanced meta-analysis methods from fixed-effects pooling through network meta-analysis? We developed a 3,276-line educational platform with 25 modules covering effect size calculation, random-effects models, heterogeneity assessment, publication bias, meta-regression, diagnostic test accuracy meta-analysis, network meta-analysis, and individual participant data methods. Each module provides worked numerical examples with step-by-step derivations, interactive parameter exploration, and quiz assessment with immediate feedback. The course was validated by checking all numerical examples against R metafor output with agreement within rounding tolerance across all 25 modules. Module quizzes included distractor options based on common misconceptions identified from published methodology surveys and teaching evaluations. A comprehensive interactive course could make advanced meta-analysis methods accessible to researchers without programming skills or software installation. The course covers methods conceptually and computationally but cannot replace supervised application with real datasets and experienced methodological mentorship.

