[249/1873] finalpaper
TITLE: Final Paper Compilation: Meta-Analytic Evidence Synthesis Manuscript Assembly
TYPE: methods  |  ESTIMAND: Manuscript completeness and reporting compliance
DATA: Compiled meta-analysis results, figures, and supplementary materials
PATH: C:\Projects\finalpaper

CURRENT BODY (154 words):
Can an automated manuscript assembly pipeline enforce reporting compliance while compiling meta-analytic results, figures, and supplements into submission-ready manuscripts? We developed a workflow assembling meta-analysis outputs including forest plots, funnel plots, summary tables, and statistical results into structured manuscript drafts following journal-specific formatting. The pipeline validates PRISMA reporting compliance by checking 27 checklist items against assembled content and flagging missing elements with remediation suggestions. Assembled manuscripts achieved 96 percent PRISMA item coverage (95% CI 89 to 99) across test compilations, with the most commonly missing items being protocol registration details and individual study risk-of-bias assessments. Automated cross-referencing between in-text citations and reference lists detected 3 orphaned references per manuscript on average. Systematic manuscript assembly could reduce formatting errors and reporting omissions that delay peer review and require revision cycles. The pipeline produces draft manuscripts requiring human review for scientific interpretation and cannot generate novel analytical content beyond what is provided in the input results.

YOUR REWRITE (at most 156 words, 7 sentences):


Can an automated pipeline enforce reporting compliance while compiling meta-analytic results into submission-ready manuscripts? We developed a compilation workflow assembling meta-analysis outputs including forest plots, funnel plots, summary tables, and statistical results into structured manuscript drafts following journal-specific formatting. The pipeline validates PRISMA reporting compliance by checking each of 27 checklist items against assembled content and flagging missing elements. Assembled manuscripts achieved 96 percent PRISMA item coverage (95% CI 89 to 99) across test compilations, with the most commonly missing items being protocol registration and individual study risk-of-bias assessments. Automated cross-referencing between in-text citations and reference lists detected 3 orphaned references per manuscript on average. Systematic manuscript assembly could reduce formatting errors and reporting omissions that delay peer review. The pipeline produces draft manuscripts requiring human review for scientific interpretation and cannot generate analytical content beyond what is provided in input results.

